{"candidates":[{"content":"SRC_005 reduces to SRC_002: the external market signal synchronization mechanism is a specific economic instance of the de facto monoculture constraint, where the shared cause (optimization metric) forces independent operators into identical failure modes. Refuted if small operators optimize for orthogonal, non-market metrics (e.g., local physical constraints) that desynchronize their scaling thresholds regardless of shared market signals.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"488eb6483c9cc218320b017cd9db9169352ae2ffe9840c602c3283aead56171a"},{"role":"dependence","target":"f4708a0202888823660314dffa00da77ab56d2254de6fb61a605d347161d35cf"},{"role":"dependence","target":"002d39c342dc2a053d1cd40089d6f9f05e812c6d4b5c3ca89d469d442ebd64b8"}],"typicality":0.85},{"content":"SRC_003 contradicts SRC_004: topological isolation (DAG structure) cannot provide superior correlated failure resilience if the observer-bias mechanism holds, because the DAG merely fragments and hides the catastrophic shared-cause failures rather than preventing them. Refuted if topological isolation provably reduces the absolute frequency of shared-cause failures (not just their observability), demonstrating causal isolation independent of the observation framework.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"c7ae1fabedc08c45ac1c396e20eab1063f3ac659ede9f70a3a56b595835edaad"},{"role":"dependence","target":"6bb7f67f71f892130004138e0ffc9d9d79674d30b86e327e86be98ec44707d97"},{"role":"dependence","target":"002d39c342dc2a053d1cd40089d6f9f05e812c6d4b5c3ca89d469d442ebd64b8"}],"typicality":0.7},{"content":"SRC_001 inherits from SRC_004: the requirement for causal firebreaks (independent codebases, power domains) is a specific implementation of the general topological requirement for a modular, loosely-coupled DAG, because causal firebreaks enforce the edges that make the dependency graph acyclic and prevent cascading failures. Refuted if a system can possess causal firebreaks while maintaining a cyclic dependency topology that still allows cascading failures under specific load conditions.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"002d39c342dc2a053d1cd40089d6f9f05e812c6d4b5c3ca89d469d442ebd64b8"},{"role":"dependence","target":"6bb7f67f71f892130004138e0ffc9d9d79674d30b86e327e86be98ec44707d97"}],"typicality":0.75},{"content":"SRC_002 abstracts SRC_005: the economic monoculture argument generalizes beyond market signal synchronization to include any isomorphic selection pressure (regulatory compliance, off-the-shelf solutions, shared training data) that collapses the causal independence of decentralized operators. Refuted if market signal synchronization induces correlated failures through a distinct dynamical mechanism (self-organized criticality) that economic monoculture arguments cannot predict or explain.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"f4708a0202888823660314dffa00da77ab56d2254de6fb61a605d347161d35cf"},{"role":"dependence","target":"488eb6483c9cc218320b017cd9db9169352ae2ffe9840c602c3283aead56171a"},{"role":"dependence","target":"002d39c342dc2a053d1cd40089d6f9f05e812c6d4b5c3ca89d469d442ebd64b8"}],"typicality":0.65},{"content":"SRC_003 shares mechanism with SRC_005: both mechanisms depend on a hidden synchronizing cause—extreme value statistics in SRC_003 and market signals in SRC_005—that induces correlated failures across 'independent' nodes while masking the correlation from observers. Refuted if the heavy-tailed distribution of catastrophic shared causes in SRC_003 operates independently of operator optimization behavior, meaning failures would correlate identically even if operators used diverse, non-convergent scaling algorithms.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"c7ae1fabedc08c45ac1c396e20eab1063f3ac659ede9f70a3a56b595835edaad"},{"role":"dependence","target":"488eb6483c9cc218320b017cd9db9169352ae2ffe9840c602c3283aead56171a"},{"role":"dependence","target":"002d39c342dc2a053d1cd40089d6f9f05e812c6d4b5c3ca89d469d442ebd64b8"}],"typicality":0.55},{"content":"SRC_004 is compatible with SRC_002: topological superiority (DAG structure) and economic superiority (engineered architectural diversity) are non-exclusive mechanisms by which a large centralized service can achieve strictly greater correlated failure resilience than a decentralized network, provided the large service enforces both internal bulkheading and diverse team practices. Refuted if enforcing a DAG topology in a decentralized network is sufficient to overcome the economic monoculture effect, making the large service's architectural diversity redundant.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"6bb7f67f71f892130004138e0ffc9d9d79674d30b86e327e86be98ec44707d97"},{"role":"dependence","target":"f4708a0202888823660314dffa00da77ab56d2254de6fb61a605d347161d35cf"},{"role":"dependence","target":"002d39c342dc2a053d1cd40089d6f9f05e812c6d4b5c3ca89d469d442ebd64b8"}],"typicality":0.45}],"simulation_proposals":[]}